  $hairstyle[$?hairstyle[&he has &?a_an[$hairstyle which is
  $haircolourfancy]][&he has a completely bald head]][&his hair is obscured by
  $]. $nosefancy[&he has @][You cannot see &his nose] and &he has
  $feature2fancy. $skintonefancy[&his skin is @][&his skin tone is hidden by $]
  and &he also has $feature1fancy.

You can use the following variables in the markup:

  &a_an[your content] - puts 'a' or 'an' and a space in front of your content as appropriate
  &?a_an[your content] - puts 'a' or 'an' and a space or nothing in front of your content based on pluralisation
  &pronoun|plural text|singular text& - alternates text based on the grammatical number of the pronoun (e.g. he/she/it vs you/they/them)
  &he - he/she/it/they/you as appropriate
  &him - him/her/it/them/you as appropriate
  &his - his/her/its/theirs/your as appropriate
  &himself - himself/herself/itself/themself/yourself as appropriate
  &male - male/female/neuter/non-binary as appropriate
  &race - the name of the character's race (human)
  &culture - the name of the character's culture (scientist)
  &ethnicity - the name of the character's ethnicity (germanic)
  &ethnicgroup - the character's ethnic group (western european)
  &personword - the character's culture's person word (scientist)
  &age - the character's age category (adult)
  &height - the character's height (1 metre 56 centimetres)
  &tattoos - a description of the character's tattoos, or blank if none ()
  &withtattoos - an alternate description of the character's tattoos, or blank if none ()
  &scars - a description of the character's scars, or blank if none ()
  &withscars - an alternate description of the character's scars, or blank if none ()

You can also use the following forms with each characteristic:

  $varname - Displays the ordinary form of the variable. See below for specifics.
  $varnamefancy - Displays the fancy form of the variable. See below for specifics.
  $varnamebasic - Displays the basic form of the variable. See below for specifics.
  $varname[display if not obscured][display if obscured] - See below for specifics.
  $?varname[display if not null/default][display if null/default] - See below for specifics.

Note: Inside the above, @ substitutes the variable description, $ substitutes for the name of the obscuring item (e.g. sunglasses), and * substitutes for the sdesc of the
obscuring item (e.g. a pair of gold-rimmed sunglasses).

The following variables can be used with the above syntax: frame, person, eyecolour, eyeshape, haircolour, hairstyle, skincolour, nose, feature1, feature2

The characteristics eyecolour and eyeshape can be used with an additional form. Inside this form, you can use @ for the variable description, % for the number of parts possessed,
* for the same but in wordy form:

  %varname[text if normal count][n-n2:text if between n and n2 count][x:text if x count][y:text if y count]
  Example: %$eyecolour[%eyecolour[$eyecolour eyes][1:one $eyecolour eye][0:no eyes]

Enter the description in the editor below.



